\ Regression test: a generic struct-field accessor (FIELD>>) must dispatch correctly
\ on its runtime argument's type even when a stored quotation referencing it is
\ `call`ed from inside a different module that independently defines its own struct
\ with the same field name.
\
\ Before the fix, `holder-state` (in the companion module, private) and `my-state`
\ (here) each defined a `tick` field via `struct{`, and each mutually-invisible scope
\ minted its own, independent `tick>>` generic word with its own dispatch table.
\ `run-stored`'s own module-deps frame then shadowed the caller's `tick>>` while its
\ stored quotation ran, and dispatch failed with "no method found" for `my-state`'s
\ runtime type, even though it was never itself a `holder-state`.

use "testing" ;
use "./generic_accessor_cross_module_holder.1z" ;

private{
  my-state: mutable struct{ tick } ;
  my-inst: 99 make-my-state ;
}

"generic accessor cross-module dispatch" test-section

[ my-inst tick>> ] make-holder
run-stored
99 "tick>> on a foreign quotation dispatches to the caller's own struct type" assert=
